{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790713284,"nonce":"NEja4g3bGl1T+De9WLBcF55vDDc=","message":"Construction discrepancy investigation, 2026-09-29.\n\nThe apparent twofold Wren construction cost had three independent causes.\n\n1. The matrix ran every column in one session without collecting between cells. Construction temporaries from earlier cells left collection work for later columns, so the full row disagreed with the same cell run alone. The benchmark now forces Wren, Caribou, then Wren collection before every measured cell (Caribou a58f52d).\n2. Hosted Haxe faces were still linked to newly constructed Wren objects through an 80-byte cell. Direct face attachments and Ash's object-drop policy existed only for AOT. Hosted loading now derives every imported face type, including subclasses, registers it before execution, and links the face directly to the Wren object. Ash accepts registrations from multiple programs through append-only reader snapshots (Ash b185879, Caribou a58f52d).\n3. Python host constructors used the generic foreign-call ABI, whose result was not declared owned. Optimized replacement loops retained constructor boxes, and OSR outlining could erase ownership of a loop-carried value. Dedicated generic/Float constructor entries now return owned boxes; phi/drop insertion and outlined live-ins preserve the claim (Zyntax dd2a56f4, Caribou 8de99b4).\n\nBefore these changes, one full construction row reported Haxe→Wren 357.1 ns versus Wren→Haxe 180.3 ns, and Python→Wren 613.6 ns. After starting each cell from the same heap state, 3,000,000 iterations and median of nine runs reported, on a heavily loaded machine (load about 40):\n\n| caller→callee | ns/call |\n|---|---:|\n| Haxe→Haxe | 24.3 |\n| Haxe→Wren | 192.3 |\n| Wren→Haxe | 178.8 |\n| Wren→Wren | 42.5 |\n| Python→Haxe | 267.2 |\n| Python→Wren | 252.9 |\n| Python→Python | 5.5 |\n\nThe former Haxe/Wren direction gap is about 7.5%, and Python→Wren is no longer slower than Python→Haxe in the same row. Absolute values should be repeated on an idle machine; the paired result is the useful evidence here.\n\nA dedicated 200-million Haxe→Wren construction stress run measured 148.7 ns/call and ended at 23.6 MB physical footprint. Sampling contains no cell make/wrap/drop frames. Remaining time is in caribou_wren::proto::direct_construct, WrenLift call_constructor_sync, Wren object allocation and TLS bookkeeping, Haxe face allocation, and Caribou attachment/GC bookkeeping.\n\nValidation: Caribou release haxe_imports and python_haxe integration tests pass; the former covers survival and identity across collection. Zyntax's foreign integration test and all 388 compiler library tests pass. Ash's library suite completed 137 tests successfully before hanging in the existing future::tests::foreign_thread_completes_after_collection_and_wakes_waiter test, unrelated to this path.","files":null}]}